From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: allolive <160342668+allolive@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 7 Oct 2026 07:12:12 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] hdmitx21: 1080p165 from a DisplayID, as a game-VRR base

Frame-sequential 3D runs the film on game VRR at a multiple of its rate
(24p at 96 Hz). The TV draws each picture as fast as the base mode sends
it: on 1080p120 that is 8 ms of a 10.4 ms frame, leaving the shutter
glasses 2.4 ms in which the screen shows one eye only. A 165 Hz base
draws it in 5.6 ms and doubles that time. TVs that take 1080p165 (an LG
G5 among them) list it in a DisplayID extension block only.

- A VESA timing HDMIV_27_1920x1080p165hz: 401 MHz, 2080 x 1169, as the
  G5's DisplayID Type I timing (reduced blanking, hsync +, vsync -).
- The EDID parser reads DisplayID 1.x Type I timings and keeps those
  that match a VESA timing of the table.
- Validity: 1080p165 passes the board's refresh limit and hdmitx21's
  VESA filter; the S6 PLL is computed from the pixel clock.
- A VESA timing sends AVI VIC 0 (the u8 took the low bits of the vic,
  another format) and is decided as RGB 8-bit in DRM too, as
  hdmitx21_set_display sends it.
- Game VRR on it: the rate is no longer capped at 120 Hz but at the base,
  rounded to the whole Hz DRM names it by; lines per frame are worked out
  in 64 bits from the pixel clock, so a VESA rate needs no table; vrr_rate
  takes "<rate> 1" for 1000/1001 of it on a base with no 1000/1001 twin
  (95.904 Hz on 1080p165), set and used under one lock and cleared by
  DRM's own hints; the carried line remainder restarts with each rate;
  struct vrr_conf_para keeps the base vic in 32 bits (a VESA vic in 8
  bits named another mode and gave ~163 lines a frame); a frame is never
  shorter than the mode's own (DRM names 164.917 Hz 165).

vinfo states the rate of the line count actually programmed: 1000/1001
of a rate is rate x 10 / 1001 (95.904 Hz exactly, where reduce_0p1_percent
gave 9590/100, 42 ppm off, and left rates not a multiple of 6 unreduced),
for any game rate on a 1000/1001 twin or given as "<rate> 1", and a rate
whose frame was clamped to the mode's line count states the mode's own
rate. A game rate needing more lines than ENCP_VIDEO_MAX_LNCNT counts
(13 bits, 8192 lines) is refused, and the handler never writes more; the
lowest accepted rate on 1080p165 needs 8043 lines, 8051 as "2397 1". The
attribute takes hdmimode_mutex before fr_hint_mutex, as
hdmitx_set_fr_hint() does.

Signed-off-by: allolive <160342668+allolive@users.noreply.github.com>
---
diff --git a/drivers/drm/meson_hdmi.c b/drivers/drm/meson_hdmi.c
index 7cf33b5..8887762 100644
--- a/drivers/drm/meson_hdmi.c
+++ b/drivers/drm/meson_hdmi.c
@@ -421,6 +421,14 @@ static int meson_hdmitx_decide_color_attr
 		}
 	}
 
+	/* VESA timings go out as RGB 8-bit (hdmitx21_set_display); decide it
+	 * here too, so the TMDS clock is worked out for what is sent
+	 */
+	if (vic >= HDMITX_VESA_OFFSET) {
+		attr->colorformat = HDMI_COLORSPACE_RGB;
+		attr->bitdepth = colordepth_to_bitdepth(COLORDEPTH_24B);
+	}
+
 	DRM_INFO("[%s]:[%s,eotf:%d,vic:%d]=>attr[%s,%dbit]\n", __func__,
 		outputmode, common->hdmi_current_eotf_type, vic,
 		colour_sampling[attr->colorformat], attr->bitdepth);
